          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which alarm features in reflux does guidance say should change the plan?",
          "answer": "Guidance on gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) indicates that certain alarm features should prompt a change in management. These alarm features include:\n\n1. Difficulty swallowing (dysphagia)\n2. Painful swallowing (odynophagia)\n3. Vomiting blood or black tarry stools (indicative of gastrointestinal bleeding)\n4. Unintentional weight loss\n5. Symptoms suggestive of a cardiac cause that may include chest discomfort associated with exertion, shortness of breath, or pain radiating to the arm, neck, or jaw.\n\nThe presence of these symptoms often requires urgent medical evaluation to rule out serious underlying conditions (source: \"When urgent assessment is advised\" in the document on gastroesophageal reflux disease).\n\nWhat I could not cover includes specific diagnostic criteria or the step-by-step management changes based on these alarm features. If you have further questions about GERD management, consult a healthcare provider.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
